            id: "viskari-2018",
            title: "Nitrogen Recovery With Source Separation of Human Urine: Preliminary Results of Its Fertiliser Potential and Use in Agriculture",
            by: "Viskari, E.-L. et al., Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems 2:32",
            url: "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/sustainable-food-systems/articles/10.3389/fsufs.2018.00032/pdf",
            published: "2018-06-28",
            read: "2026-10-23",
            kind: Primary,
            registry: Some("peer-reviewed-literature"),
            note: "Read the PDF text. Table 1 gives N and P in urine, faeces and other household wastewater per person per year (citing Weckman 2005, Udert et al. 2006, Ministry of the Environment Finland 2017), so these are Finnish values for a high-protein diet. The text also quotes Vinneras and Jonsson (2002): about 3.7 kg N, 0.34 kg P and 1.2 kg K per person a year recoverable from urine. It also repeats Cordell et al.'s 2009 claim of 50 to 100 years of easily extractable phosphate rock, which usgs-mcs-2026-phosphate contradicts (see earth_today.ron).",
        ),
        (
            id: "cordell-2009-secondary",
            title: "The story of phosphorus: Global food security and food for thought (Global Environmental Change 19(2):292-305), as cited",
            by: "Cordell, D., Drangert, J.-O. and White, S.; cited by a SuSanA summary and a symposium paper",
